# Crewman Chatter
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistcrewman-line-1 = Four generations of my family served aboard one transport. Grandfather left me his bunk, his tools and his argument with the storekeeper.
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistcrewman-line-2 = Thirty air recyclers in the hold. All working, all inspected, and all agree to leave the smell of machine oil alone.
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistcrewman-line-3 = A Rogue Trader can discover a new world. We still unload the crates for that discovery.
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistcrewman-line-4 = After crossing the Maw, familiar sounds are a comfort. Even the chief boatswain. For about five minutes.
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistcrewman-line-5 = Checked the third deck seals. Now checking that nobody has put me down to check them again.
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistcrewman-line-6 = On Footfall you can go ashore and still never see a sky. Convenient. Very little adjustment needed.

# Armsman Chatter
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistguard-line-1 = Keep the passage clear. The loaders already think their crates own the corridor.
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistguard-line-2 = I clean the AKMS after every watch. Dust reaches this outpost before the deliveries do.
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistguard-line-3 = The captain pays on time. We hand over on time too, though the boatswain's clock always seems fast.
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistguard-line-4 = Ask the purser about purchases, the officer about paperwork. I can tell you where the queue ends.
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistguard-line-5 = Crates are checked against the manifest. "It rolled over to me" is not an entry on the manifest yet.
wh40k-footfall-ambient-chartistguard-line-6 = Helmet fastened, post accepted. Now to wait for my relief to confuse "one minute" with a whole watch again.

# Officer Dialogue
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-intro = Severin Holt, officer of the Chartist trading mission. I oversee unloading and ship papers. If you arrived without a crate blocking the passage, we have made a promising start.
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-menu = What would you like to know? While the loaders check their manifests, I have a few minutes.
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-lore-charter-choice = About the Charter
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-lore-charter-reply = A Charter defines a captain's right to carry cargo and passengers along established routes. Behind it stand seals, obligations and generations of service. We carry grain, promethium, machinery and people: everything that keeps fine crests from hanging over empty warehouses. Father called a timely delivery the best form of patriotism. A few hungry ports persuaded me he was right.
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-lore-roguetraders-choice = About Rogue Traders
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-lore-roguetraders-reply = A Rogue Trader's Warrant permits far greater freedom beyond Imperial borders. Our service is bound to a Charter and its routes. They seek new opportunities; we sustain the traffic. Arguing over which matters more makes good dinner conversation. When the fuel runs out, that argument soon becomes an order.
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-lore-maw-choice = Traversing the Maw
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-lore-maw-reply = The Maw connects Calixis and the Koronus Expanse through dangerous warp routes. Navigators check the passage, tech-priests check the Geller fields, and the crew prepares the ship. Good charts command good prices: you cannot correct a mistake here with a fresh manifest. After a crossing, I listen to engineering's report first. Then I allow myself to breathe.
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-lore-macrotransports-choice = Macro-transports
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-lore-macrotransports-reply = A great transport is a city of decks, workshops and family histories. Some crews serve for generations; a child learns the route to engineering before ever seeing an open sky. The ship is their home. Asking them to "just move a few bulkheads" can start an argument several centuries older than the request.
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-lore-footfall-choice = Footfall Outpost
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-lore-footfall-reply = Our mission on Footfall maintains stores and contact with the transports. Livia handles provisions, the watch keeps the passage clear, and I sort the papers. Order takes patience here: someone else's cargo becomes "abandoned" remarkably quickly if left unattended.
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistofficer-farewell = Safe passage. Check your cargo before departure; a prayer does not put a seal on a manifest.

# Merchant Dialogue
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistmerchant-intro = Livia Dorn, ship's purser. I can help you provision without excess weight. If you need three dress uniforms for one expedition, I will still ask why.
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistmerchant-menu = Show you the current stock, or explain what we bring in?
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistmerchant-lore-goods-choice = About goods
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistmerchant-lore-goods-reply = We bring fleet uniforms, protective gear, AKMS magazines, oxygen and long-life rations. Part of each delivery reaches the counter; the rest goes to our crews. Check the current manifest: yesterday's conversation will not reserve the last box. Check the caliber before paying too. Convictions do not fit in a chamber.
wh40k-footfall-dialogue-chartistmerchant-farewell = Come back before our next departure. Leave room in your pack for whatever you are actually sailing to find.
